\section*{Complex matrix multiplication at exponent nine-fourths}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 107; 2026-10-02), the asserted result is that the complex matrix-multiplication exponent is at most $9/4$.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Manuscript 1.}
For every $\varepsilon>0$, the claim gives an exact complex-arithmetic algorithm multiplying two $n\times n$ matrices in
\[
 O_{\varepsilon}(n^{9/4+\varepsilon})\quad\text{operations},\qquad\omega\le9/4.
\]
Its associated tensor inequality uses $T=\sum_{i=1}^sT_i$ with every $T_i\ne0$, a shared first tensor factor and matched disjoint sectors on the other two. A tensor character $\lambda$ takes nonnegative real values and is additive, multiplicative and restriction-monotone, with $\lambda(1)=1$; define $p_X$ by $\lambda(x\sum_{j=1}^my_jz_j)=m^{p_X}$. For every probability vector $q$ the claimed inequality is
\[
 \lambda(T)\ge e^{p_XH(q)}\prod_i\lambda(T_i)^{q_i},\qquad
 H(q)=-\sum_iq_i\log q_i,
\]
with $0\log0=0$. These are exact arithmetic and tensor-character statements, without finite-precision assumptions.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 107; 2026-10-02), tensor degenerations separate blocks that already expose two independent variable legs. Character inequalities for polynomial multiplication, a determinant filtration, and discrete growth bound the detecting character values for matrix multiplication at the claimed exponent.

